What Immune cuts out of command lines before it keeps or prints them.

`gitleaks/` holds gitleaks's published patterns, with their own NOTICE.

`command-line.json` holds the forms gitleaks does not recognise on a command line, as data:

- `flag_words` from `datadog`: the default sensitive words of the Datadog Agent's process
  data scrubber, `defaultSensitiveWords` in `pkg/process/procutil` of
  https://github.com/DataDog/datadog-agent, licensed under the Apache License 2.0
  (https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0), copyright 2016-present Datadog, Inc. The words
  are taken as Datadog writes them, `*` standing for any run of characters. How a word is matched
  against a flag's name is Immune's own (`src/secrets.rs`), not Datadog's.
- `flag_words` from `immune`: three words of Immune's own that Datadog's list lacks.
- `detect-secrets:basic-auth`: the regular expression of `BasicAuthDetector` in Yelp's
  detect-secrets, `detect_secrets/plugins/basic_auth.py` in https://github.com/Yelp/detect-secrets,
  licensed under the Apache License 2.0, with its reserved characters `:/?#[]@` filled in as that
  code fills them. It is changed in two ways, so that more is cut: the user may be empty, as in
  `redis://:<password>@host`, and the password may hold `:` and `@`, so it runs to the last `@`
  before the host.
- `immune:authorization` and `immune:mysql-password`: rules of Immune's own, for an
  `Authorization` header written without quotes, as Endpoint Security gives an argument, and for
  a MySQL or MariaDB client's password joined to `-p`. No published pattern fits these on a
  command line. Each names its key, and where the value after it ends is decided by Immune's
  code, as for a flag's value (`value_follows`).

`SHA256SUMS` holds the sha256 of `command-line.json` and of `gitleaks/gitleaks.json` as they are
here. A test fails when either differs from it, and the program checks both sums each time it
loads the set and loads nothing when one differs.
